 {"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-48", "title": "normalize-board --apply silently DROPS a task row created while it runs — the write lock wraps only the rewrite, not the read/classify — and validate/doctor stay green while the row's task_created event is left orphaned", "status": "in_progress", "priority": "P1", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 25 (tick boardctl-dogfood-2026-10-23-10-52-34), SECOND delivery of the tick; reproduced at HEAD 900b7b5 (built from a clean checkout copy, `boardctl version dev`) AND on the RELEASED v0.1.11 binary — identical behaviour, so this is product code, not deployment drift.\n\nPROBE (decisive, both binaries): board with 20,000 drifted rows (so --apply has work). CONTROL: `create --id CTRL-1` with no apply running -> rc=0, row present. TREATMENT: start `normalize-board --apply`, wait 0.6s (apply already past its read), run `create --id PROBE-MID-1` -> rc=0 'created task PROBE-MID-1 (appended to .../tasks.jsonl)', row IS present in tasks.jsonl while the apply is still running, and GONE after the apply finishes. apply rc=0, prints a successful 'applied' rewrite. events.jsonl keeps the probe's task_created event (1 match) while tasks.jsonl has 0 -> orphan event. Bulk rows intact 20000/20000, so the loss is specific to the concurrent append, not general damage.\n\nREPEATED: 8 rounds x 20 concurrent creates = 160 creates, every one rc=0 'created task', 0 present afterwards (unparseable lines 0, duplicate ids 0). A fleet lane writing while an operator/foreman runs the repair loses every row it wrote during the window, silently.\n\nMECHANISM (source, both same at HEAD and in the v0.1.11 tag): internal/board/normalizeboard.go NormalizeBoard(apply) reads tasks.jsonl and classifies every line FIRST (the read/classify pass ends at the rewritten-bytes assembly around line 400), and only then acquires the write guard at normalizeboard.go:418-421, which wraps nothing but atomicRewrite(tasksPath, rewritten). So the lock covers the write, not the check-then-act window. internal/board/lock.go:17-19 states the guard exists precisely to prevent this ('read-modify-write rewrites ... silently drop the winner's change'), and README:1256-1259 tells users 'every write verb takes an exclusive advisory flock ... for its whole check-then-act window'. For this verb the documentation and the code disagree.\n\nSILENCE: after the loss, `validate` -> rc=0, 'RESULT: OK (0 warning(s))', 20002 rows; `doctor` -> rc=0. Nothing reconciles events against rows, so the operator sees a green board that is missing a row they watched being created.\n\nWINDOW SCALES WITH BOARD SIZE (hyperfine --warmup 1 --runs 5, HEAD build, `normalize-board --apply` re-applied each run): 1,000 rows 16.0 ms +/- 5.8; 10,000 rows 84.3 ms +/- 9.4; 50,000 rows 346.2 ms +/- 25.6. On a real second-scale repair of a big board the window is not a theoretical race.\n\nTEST GAP: internal/board/write_concurrency_test.go has 7 tests and every one is append-vs-append, same-id racing, lock exclusivity/release, or reader isolation. No test interleaves a rewrite with an append, which is why this shipped.\n\nRepro artifacts (this tick's scratch, discarded): /tmp/df1023/repro.sh (decisive control/treatment), /tmp/df1023/conc.sh (8x20 concurrent), /tmp/df1023/measure_rewrite.sh (the sizing numbers).", "created_at": "2026-10-23T11:26:21Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-23T13:02:35Z", "created_by": null, "acceptance_criteria": "1) A create that lands while a rewrite verb is running is still present after the verb exits: the read/classify pass for NormalizeBoard (and RepairEvents, DedupeByID) runs INSIDE the same write guard as its write, or the rewrite re-reads the file under the guard and merges rows it did not classify (no lost append). 2) A regression test in internal/board/write_concurrency_test.go interleaves an append with each rewrite verb and asserts the appended row/event survives; the test FAILS on the current tree and passes after the fix (prove both directions). 3) The same test asserts no orphan task_created event is produced. 4) If serializing the whole verb is rejected as a design choice, the command must instead detect a concurrent append (the byte-comparison refusal internal/board/repair.go:129-142 already implements) and refuse with a precise message, and README:1256-1259 must stop claiming the lock covers 'its whole check-then-act window'. 5) `go test ./...` exits 0.", "capability_tags": [], "complexity": 3, "depends_on": [], "foreman_note": null, "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": "Wave dispatch staged; worker running.", "detail": {"fingerprint": "adda5f2a36866c988dbbcc959507b8bbcf5463cf3bcdc5cd7e1ba97a28d37e44", "evidence": [{"run_id": "boardctl-dogfood-2026-10-23-10-52-34", "ts": "2026-10-23T11:26:21Z"}]}, "worker_status": "in_progress", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-DF-BOARDCTL-48", "branch": "wt/DF-BOARDCTL-48"}
-{"id": "DF-BOARDCTL-49", "title": "normalize-board --repair-events --apply drops EVENTS appended while it runs (5/5 attempts on both the released v0.1.11 and HEAD) and rewrites events.jsonl in place instead of atomically; dedupe-board's DedupeByID has the same unlocked read-then-rewrite shape", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "Dogfood run 25 (tick boardctl-dogfood-2026-10-23-10-52-34), second delivery. Same root cause as DF-BOARDCTL-48 on the events side.\n\nPROBE (/tmp/df1023/repro_events3.sh): events.jsonl seeded with 60,000 events carrying descending ids (so the repair has work); `normalize-board --repair-events --apply` started, then after 0.10s `event --type audit --tick 999 --detail-text PROBE-EV-<n> --no-tick-write` appended (rc=0, 'event id N appended'). Final file read back: the probe detail is ABSENT in 5/5 attempts on the HEAD build and 5/5 on the released v0.1.11 binary (apply rc=0 each time, 0.52-0.73s wall). A successful append that is not in the final file is an overwritten write; nothing reports it.\n\nMECHANISM: internal/board/repair_events.go RepairEvents(false) parses and renumbers every event before the guard, then acquires the write guard only around `os.WriteFile(b.eventsPath, out, 0o644)` (repair_events.go:209-214). The rewrite bytes come from the pre-append snapshot, so any event appended in between is discarded. Two aggravators: (a) that write is a plain os.WriteFile, not atomicRewrite(tmp+rename) as normalize-board uses, so a crash mid-write can truncate the whole event trail; (b) dedupe-board's DedupeByID calls atomicRewrite at internal/board/iddupe.go:234 with the read and planning above it (iddupe.go:107-236) and NO write guard anywhere in that function — the only guard in the file is in CloseIDDupeLine (iddupe.go:329). Same shape, not probed here because cmd/dedupe-board is a one-shot source-only tool; listed so the fix covers it in one pass.\n\nContext for severity: events.jsonl is the audit trail the fleet reads back ('who closed what, when'), and README documents the repair as a safe operator action on a live board.", "created_at": "2026-10-23T11:26:21Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-23T11:26:21Z", "created_by": null, "acceptance_criteria": "1) An event appended while the repair runs must survive it: start `boardctl normalize-board --repair-events --apply` on a 60,000-event fixture with descending ids, then run `boardctl event --type audit --tick 999 --detail-text PROBE-EV-1 --no-tick-write` while it is running; after the apply exits, `grep -c PROBE-EV-1 events.jsonl` must print 1 (today it prints 0). 2) events.jsonl must be replaced atomically: kill -9 the process mid-rewrite and `grep -c` of a seeded marker in events.jsonl must print the same count as before the run, i.e. the previous file is still intact (not truncated). 3) internal/board/iddupe.go's DedupeByID must either take the same write guard around its read-then-atomicRewrite window or carry a comment stating why it is exempt: `sed -n '/func.*DedupeByID/,/^}/p' internal/board/iddupe.go` prints the guard call or the exemption comment. 4) A regression test interleaving an append with the repair must exit 1 before the fix and exit 0 after it: `go test ./internal/board -run Concurrent`. 5) Full suite exits 0: `go test ./...` expected exit code 0.", "capability_tags": [], "complexity": 3, "depends_on": [], "foreman_note": null, "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "039d15d3139fbad6944d11284380ea364dade3c6dde60fd343590cbd1099b88e", "evidence": [{"run_id": "boardctl-dogfood-2026-10-23-10-52-34", "ts": "2026-10-23T11:26:21Z"}]}}
